From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 25 Jun 2026 10:26:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03970/11362] [outer-loop] Correct L2 truncation-fix note per
 adversarial review: retract isolation "satisfiable" over-claim

An adversarial verification pass (workflow verify-l2-truncation-fix) caught 3 over-claims in the
first draft. Corrections:
- RETRACT "satisfiable goal in isolation": the synthetic test was circular (prompt literally
  instructs is_level_complete must return True + prints the grid -> model copies it), and the
  harness's real _goal_satisfiability_check (BFS reachability over the induced engine) would return
  False anyway (toy engine only mutates cell [2,2]; hardcoded 25-cell grid unreachable). Defensible
  isolation claim is ONLY "emits valid parseable code, fast".
- Attribute stopping to the stop=["```"] sequence, NOT the code-only directive (directive alone ran
  to the 4096 limit, 605s; stop-seq -> 195 tokens / 15.6s).
- Back the live conclusion with the fix-enabled run's binary arm (logs/proto_graded_ab_codeonly.log:
  lp85/binary maxL=1 exemplar=True gps=False), not the stale pre-fix proto_graded artifact. exemplar=True
  confirms the unsatisfiable goal is the L2 attempt (not a benign L1 placeholder); the fix moves the
  live failure from "no code (truncated)" to "valid code, inconsistent/unsatisfiable goal" -> the
  deeper wall is goal-induction QUALITY. lp85 L2 still does NOT bank.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
